            <div xml:id="d535">

                <pb facs="0673" n="553" sameAs="frus:frus1931v02/pg553"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.90f2/7: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Dawes</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-10-26T12:00:00+00:00">October 26,
                        1931—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received October 26—9:15 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>421. Am informed by Hejazi Minister that his Government has insisted in all
                    treaties so far concluded upon the inclusion of a clause <pb facs="0674" n="554"
                        xml:id="pg554"/> stating in substance “Arabic and (English) texts shall both
                    be official texts and both have equal value.”</p>
                <p>Hejazi Minister foresees this point will be immediately raised by his Government
                    and to avoid delay asks your concurrence that such a phrase should be inserted
                    in the agreement contemplated in the Department’s 953, October 12, 1931.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dawes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
